<!--Post: 3349--><!--Time: 1104817341-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3349"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 03, 2005 - 9:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#40;lol&#41;  Thanks, J! <BR> <BR>I don&#39;t think the message is for us, quite frankly.  But if I&#39;m reading this right, BTK&#39;s joke is.  The wordplay is classic BTK.  He never had any playmates named Petra and Tina.  He had a pet rat in a cage.  Which certainly fits in with my contention that his 2004 confessions are mostly bogus.  I imagine he had major, colossal hoot, rotfl, listening to Wichita&#39;s finest reading off those bogus names on TV.  Petra and Tina - just realistic enough to have us chasing our tails all over the net. <BR> <BR>Sadly, that &#40;speculative&#41; pet rat may have treated him better than a lot of other folks - adults and children - probably did. <BR> <BR>Point for BTK.  And two points to Tom for catching it.  The BTK crypto club loses a point, but gains insight, validating some theories toward the puzzle.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
